Let <math>\text{E}</math> be the bulk modulus of elasticity, defined as increase of pressure ÷ decrease of volume per unit volume where the pressure increase is so small that this ratio is constant, <math>\omega</math> the small increase of pressure, and <math>-(dy/dx)</math> the volume ''decrease'', then
{{block center|<math>\text{E} = \omega/(-dy/dx) \text{ or } \omega/\text{E} = -dy/dx</math> (1)}}
This gives the relation between pressure excess and displacement.

To find the relation of the velocity to displacement and pressure we shall express the fact that the wave travels on carrying all its conditions with it, so that the displacement now at <math>\text{M}</math> will arrive at <math>\text{N}</math> while the wave travels over <math>\text{MN}</math>. Let <math>\text{U}</math> be the velocity of the wave and let <math>u</math> be the velocity of the particle originally at <math>\text{N}</math>. Let <math>\text{MN} = dx = \text{U}dt</math>. In the time <math>dt</math> which the wave takes to travel over <math>\text{MN}</math> the particle displacement at <math>\text{N}</math> changes by <math>\text{QR}</math>, and <math>\text{QR} = -udt</math>, so that <math>\text{QR}/\text{MN} = -u/\text{U}</math>. But <math>\text{QR}/\text{MN} = dy/dx</math>. Then
{{block center|<math>u/\text{U} = -dy/dx</math> (2)}}
This gives the velocity of any particle in terms of the displacement. Equating (1) and (2)
